[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125162,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital dermal melanocytosis (CDM) is an increased number of melanocytes in the dermis, usually on the lower back and buttocks.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125163,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125164,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LC10 Dermal melanocytosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125165,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> Mongolian spot.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125166,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125167,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common site is the sacral area (affected in 78.9% of cases) {23834326}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125168,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CDM manifests as a bluish or darker patch {23834326}. It can be associated with inborn errors of metabolism, including GM1 gangliosidoses, mucopolysaccharidosis type&nbsp;I (Hurler syndrome) and type&nbsp;II (Hunter syndrome), mucolipidosis, Niemann&ndash;Pick disease, mannosidosis {24340274}, cleft lip/palate, supernumerary digits, benign leptomeningeal melanocytoma, lysosomal storage disease, and Sj&ouml;gren&ndash;Larsson syndrome {8157782; 12873889; 17300667}. Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis is the association of extensive, aberrant, persistent CDM with vascular anomalies, such as naevus flammeus, Sturge&ndash;Weber syndrome, or Klippel&ndash;Tr&eacute;naunay syndrome {17937434; 18045734}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125169,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CDMs are most common in Asian and African populations {572849; 7028357; 17172067; 23834326; 24340274; 31141213}. There is no sex predilection {6997352; 20653863}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125170,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125171,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unclear. It has been proposed that dermal melanosis arises from an arrest of migration of melanocytes, derived from neural crest cells, which become trapped in the dermis instead of reaching the epidermis {22197860}. <em>GNAQ</em> and <em>GNA11</em> mutations have been found in cases associated with phakomatosis pigmentovascularis {26778290}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125172,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125173,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>CDMs are composed of single pigmented dendritic melanocytes sparsely dispersed in the dermis, often parallel to the epidermis {13130904}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The melanocytes express melanocytic markers such as SOX10, melan-A, or HMB45.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Naevus of Ito and naevus of Ota have similar histology but different anatomical localization. Blue naevi tend to be more cellular and have bundles of dendritic cells {22197860}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125174,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125175,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125176,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> bluish patches on lumbosacral skin and other parts of the body; presence of dendritic melanocytes in the dermis, without atypical features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125177,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125178,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 78,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CDMs typically disappear by the age of 5&ndash;6&nbsp;years. Involvement of other sites, multiple patches, size &gt;&nbsp;100&nbsp;mm, and dark colour are associated with persistent CDMs {23834326}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]